00:09:38,320 --> 00:09:39,600
So tell me what that is.

143
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:42,080
Yeah, so excited about the Engage SDK as well.

144
00:09:42,240 --> 00:09:43,440
It's a pretty big shift for us.

145
00:09:43,600 --> 00:09:51,920
And I should mention that I've been working on this team for several years and have seen Google Play go through so many stages of evolution and maturity.

146
00:09:52,080 --> 00:09:53,680
So I'm so blessed that I've seen that.

147
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:57,040
And now more than ever., we're very focused on apps.

148
00:09:57,280 --> 00:10:02,400
We have an apps GM, Maca, who spoke in our play keynote yesterday, along with other incredible speakers.

149
00:10:02,640 --> 00:10:12,640
We also are really thinking about the various categories within apps and how we develop meaningful experiences, go a little deeper than maybe just generic apps as a category.

150
00:10:12,960 --> 00:10:26,000
So the Engage SDK was born from this research we found where so many users have apps on their phone in the multiples of tens, typically above 50, above 80 in many markets, and they're just not using them as regularly as they should.

151
00:10:26,320 --> 00:10:27,520
I think we can all relate to this.

152
00:10:27,840 --> 00:10:29,360
You've got just like a lot, a lot of icons.

153
00:10:29,360 --> 00:10:33,760
And at some point, you probably had a very relevant use case and then you forget to go back.

154
00:10:33,920 --> 00:10:38,880
Maybe even the same problem comes up in your life again, but you just kind of be, oh, I forgot that I installed that app, you know, six months ago.

155
00:10:39,120 --> 00:10:44,480
And in talking to partners and developers like my team does, many developers don't have a use case that needs to be daily.

156
00:10:44,560 --> 00:10:46,720
You know, some apps are daily use and that's the intention.

157
00:10:46,880 --> 00:10:47,760
Some will not be daily.

158
00:10:47,920 --> 00:10:50,640
Or if they are daily, you might just miss some of that content.

159
00:10:51,280 --> 00:10:53,520
So we knew that was a problem we wanted to help solve.

160
00:10:53,680 --> 00:10:55,440
And we thought through like, what does that look like?

161
00:10:55,600 --> 00:10:56,720
What could we be doing differently?

162
00:10:57,120 --> 00:11:07,680
And so the Engage SDK is a way for developers to share with us on device in a very personalized and private way content that's relevant to the user on that device.

163
00:11:07,840 --> 00:11:19,760
So for you, you know, your feed in YouTube might be one example or what you recently bought in a shopping app, that kind of thing to just provide you a little bit of contextual information and gather that up in clusters that make sense.

164
00:11:20,400 --> 00:11:41,120
depending on the use case if it's a social use case or shopping whatever it might be that we know users tend to kind of think about their apps in different groups so that's what it was born out of we announced at io that it's now in developer preview and anyone can go follow the link and start building for it which we're very excited for and we already have some fantastic partners that we announced on stage spotify uber eats mcdonalds's, TikTok, many others.

165
00:11:41,440 --> 00:11:44,320
And so I'm super pumped of how this is going to evolve.

166
00:11:44,720 --> 00:11:58,160
Really, our mission is to bring that content that you're all building, all the developers that are listening to this, to the forefront and show contextual personal information on a regular basis so that users don't have to go hunt in their wall of 80 icons.

167
00:11:58,640 --> 00:12:00,960
And instead, it will just come to them in this experience.

168
00:12:01,120 --> 00:12:03,520
So very excited for what we're doing with Engage SDK.

169
00:12:03,840 --> 00:12:09,120
So I got a little sneak peek and I know it's not coming until later this year, but I'm excited.

170
00:12:09,680 --> 00:12:14,880
Also, again, another thing that makes me want to build an Android app because this looks really cool and things like TikTok.

171
00:12:15,280 --> 00:12:18,400
and Twitter and YouTube, they have this natural draw.

172
00:12:18,640 --> 00:12:24,000
You're standing in line at a grocery store and you want to kill some time and you get drawn into those things.

173
00:12:24,240 --> 00:12:32,640
But of course, more and more as a society, we realize that's probably not the best thing for a human to just be consuming dopamine hits constantly.

174
00:12:32,960 --> 00:12:42,000
And what's really cool about the way this is designed and the way it's going to be surfaced throughout the OS is that it maybe kind of breaks that chain a little bit.

175
00:12:42,240 --> 00:12:47,200
There's an app, Headway, that has these really great ads like Stop Doom scrolling and learn instead.

176
00:12:47,760 --> 00:12:56,720
They kind of summarize books and it's a simple easy way to get some bite size insights that actually benefit your life versus like doomstrolling TikTok or whatever.

177
00:12:56,800 --> 00:12:57,920
I mean, TikTok's a great app.

178
00:12:58,080 --> 00:12:59,680
I don't want to totally throw them under the bus.

179
00:13:00,080 --> 00:13:02,960
But I think a lot of us find we engage too much with those.

180
00:13:03,360 --> 00:13:17,120
What excites me about this is like it's going to surface apps and context and features that maybe people should be interacting with more, but that don't have that kind of natural draw that you're standing in line and think to do.

181
00:13:17,920 --> 00:13:36,080
And so I think this is going to be a really great feature, not just for developers to get people back into their apps, but also just to help people diversifyify their experience and maybe get some of those more meaningful experiences on their device brought to them and having those triggers to go back to those things instead of getting lost in the sea of apps and not even remembering?

182
00:13:36,240 --> 00:13:36,640
Absolutely.

183
00:13:36,720 --> 00:13:39,440
I mean, we definitely, our goal is to help users be more intentional.

184
00:13:39,520 --> 00:13:44,640
It's really part of Google's mission is very much about access to information, useful information, at scale.

185
00:13:45,040 --> 00:14:03,840
And this is one way where instead of you on autopilot, kind of like you mentioned, going to your phone and maybe just missing out on so much of the additional things you can do there and maybe just default to the same behaviors, what if you see everything that might be relevant to you that maybe just wouldn't be tal be top of mind to your point because of just the nature of that app not being as frequent potentially.

186
00:14:04,320 --> 00:14:05,280
And you are in control.

187
00:14:05,600 --> 00:14:09,120
You can see what you want to access, what's relevant to you, and then go a level deeper.

188
00:14:09,280 --> 00:14:19,680
And again, back to my team and what we care about making sure developers are getting that benefit and seeing that traffic back into their apps, also having full access and control over what is shown to the user.

189
00:14:19,840 --> 00:14:21,120
It's very personal on device.

190
00:14:21,440 --> 00:14:32,080
Yeah, I do think there's some strong element of intentionality here to help users really use their phone as a supercomputer and take advantage of the full corpus of insight and data and services on it.

