383
00:31:06,000 --> 00:31:14,000
There's so much that we think we know about how users want to pay or how apps should be built, and then we just, you know, it gets turned on our head sometimes, which is a really wonderful thing.

384
00:31:14,240 --> 00:31:15,600
So, yes, you're spot on.

385
00:31:15,840 --> 00:31:35,200
I think in what we found in Asia Pacific, more users are interested in a diverse way of paying, usually IAP, often a blend of subscriptions and IAP, whereas in the US, it typically has been the case that people launch with subscriptions businesses and scale those, maybe offer more plans, but rarely will branch out into IAP in addition.

386
00:31:35,680 --> 00:31:46,960
And the interesting thing is that the developers who are based out of Asia Pacific and who have been more willing to experiment with subscriptions and IAP actually tend to find really good success in the US.

387
00:31:47,680 --> 00:31:54,080
So, you know, it's a really little known fact that the US is one of the largest mid to entry level device markets for Android.

388
00:31:54,160 --> 00:31:58,560
It's, you know, there's this mindset that the US is, you know, everyone's on a premium device, but that's just not true.

389
00:31:58,880 --> 00:32:07,120
So we do find that developers who are willing to experiment with either a hybrid of IAP and subscriptionstions or more IP will tend to actually do very well in the US.

390
00:32:07,360 --> 00:32:17,200
There are more people with different devices, different preferences who might not be willing to pay for an annual subscription, but they might do a seven day pass or a standalone consumables for a specific product.

391
00:32:17,520 --> 00:32:20,880
So yeah, we do see this big regional difference and I'm glad you mentioned the white paper.

392
00:32:21,120 --> 00:32:23,920
Very proud of the team, our consulting team that worked on that.

393
00:32:24,080 --> 00:32:28,880
We have really wonderful researchers and consultants who go deeper into these topics.

394
00:32:29,440 --> 00:32:34,240
So I want to dive deeper into diversifying revenue streams and hybrid monetization.

395
00:32:34,480 --> 00:32:38,080
But before we get there, I did want to take a step back and talk about how.

396
00:32:38,320 --> 00:32:42,160
how different the Play Store is than iOS.

397
00:32:42,640 --> 00:32:52,480
And I think too many people it's like they know it's different, but there's still kind of a tendency to copy and paste their iOS strategy and think it's going to work on Android.

398
00:32:52,960 --> 00:32:57,360
And this is part of it, is the diversification of payments, but the Play Store works differently.

399
00:32:57,600 --> 00:33:00,880
The people are different, the devices are different, the willingness to pay is different.

400
00:33:01,040 --> 00:33:04,880
Like there's some pretty big differences between the iOS market and the Android market.

401
00:33:05,360 --> 00:33:14,480
And when you're on both platforms, like a lot of developers who say they can't find success on Android, some of that is maybe that they aren't spending enough time to really understand those differences.

402
00:33:14,880 --> 00:33:17,280
Any key things that you think developers should be thinking about?

403
00:33:17,840 --> 00:33:18,720
Yeah, absolutely.

404
00:33:18,880 --> 00:33:21,840
I mean, this is a really big topic and something that we talk a lot about on the team.

405
00:33:22,080 --> 00:33:27,600
How can we reduce those barriers to understanding and entry and help developers to build for play in a different way?

406
00:33:27,760 --> 00:33:32,320
And I think one thing we've noticed in our data is there's so many devices on Android.

407
00:33:32,560 --> 00:33:43,920
There's a huge spectrum of you've got the $2,000 device, you know, the latest S device from Samsung or a $200 device that somebody got and it works very well, but it doesn't have the same spec as a very high end device.

408
00:33:44,080 --> 00:33:48,080
And the users who are on the more premium devices actually behave quite similarly to.

409
00:33:48,480 --> 00:33:52,160
to those on iOS or other premium SKU heavy platforms.

410
00:33:52,720 --> 00:33:58,000
So if you're only targeting those users, then potentially the same behaviors will work with some swaps to the features.

411
00:33:58,080 --> 00:34:05,680
But if you're really targeting a premium user, there might be one SKU that you build for that or you just generally keep your aperture a little more narrow.

412
00:34:06,160 --> 00:34:15,840
But that will not get you the full benefit and the beauty of play on Android where you've got 2.5 billion people around the world and many for whom this is their first computing device, their only computing device.

413
00:34:16,320 --> 00:34:18,000
And their preferences are going to look different.

414
00:34:18,400 --> 00:34:25,760
And so we absolutely do encourage anyone building for play on Android to think bigger and to think about the bigger diversity of users that you can reach.

415
00:34:26,160 --> 00:34:36,960
So whether it's, I think really it comes down to the full funnel, thinking through how am I going to target my message, how am I going to offer different features and services and value that can really appeal more broadly.

416
00:34:37,120 --> 00:34:46,080
It doesn't mean that you have to offer too many bells and whistles, you know, like every single thing in the app, but tailor that depending on device type, on region, and especially...

417
00:34:46,880 --> 00:35:04,560
price points and different skews whether it's subscriptions or ip even just different plans depending on the user and what your signals are telling yourself that they might want so i think that's really critical and we've seen that in our our work, that the developers who take that time and do that little extra legroom to understand, like, oh, this is a different platform.

418
00:35:04,960 --> 00:35:06,800
It's a bit more diverse, there's more to do.

419
00:35:07,280 --> 00:35:09,360
They reap a lot more rewards than those that don't.

420
00:35:09,840 --> 00:35:12,960
So I want to dive deeper into a lot of things you mentioned.

421
00:35:13,360 --> 00:35:18,240
And you mentioned specifically that, and we've been talking about this, like diversification of revenue streams.

422
00:35:18,400 --> 00:35:20,800
And there's a lot of different ways that can happen.

423
00:35:21,440 --> 00:35:27,920
And so one of the top things, and not only are there a lot of ways that can happen, there's kind of stages to it as well.

424
00:35:28,880 --> 00:35:37,120
And so for a small team, thinking about all the different skew, and we'll get to that in and talk more deeply about how to package all the different SKUs and stuff.

425
00:35:37,360 --> 00:35:46,480
But even for the early developers, the lowest hanging fruit is to experiment with pricing and packaging and maybe even layering in some consumables.

426
00:35:47,040 --> 00:35:54,080
How do you think about how developers should start the kind of baby steps to like expanding into the diversification of revenue?

427
00:35:54,320 --> 00:36:08,400
It's a great point because, yeah, if you're a subscription developer who has maybe one tier, potentially two, but kind of you've got like one plan, a premium plan, and that's what you're working with, jumping straight from that to like, let me build a virtual currency ecosystem might be quite a bit to take on.

428
00:36:08,400 --> 00:36:08,560
It's going to be quite a lot to take on.

429
00:36:08,720 --> 00:36:10,240
It's possible, but it's not going to be easy.

430
00:36:10,480 --> 00:36:11,200
You're spot on that.

431
00:36:11,280 --> 00:36:16,000
We definitely recommend the low hanging fruit of testing pricing and tiers is a good place to start.

432
00:36:16,160 --> 00:36:34,960
And that's something that we often see, for example, especially in content heavy spaces, so media entertainment, books, music, that there's some anxiety to go outside the boundaries of this premium plan, a lot to do with the unit economics of the content that you might be providing as a developer, a lot to do with just norms of competitors.

433
00:36:35,440 --> 00:36:42,480
But what we find is that if you can just think about what works for you, it's not what your competitor is doing or what another industry is doing might not be the right fit.

434
00:36:43,120 --> 00:37:20,000
to start to test to pick apart you know what might be a better fit for a seven day pack like a starter pack for your app if you're a music app or an audio app maybe offering like a few podcasts as a consumable and if that works and people want more then maybe thinking through okay how might this fit into my broader premium plan so absolutely agree that if you're early to this experimentation phase to first start with pricing and plans and see what works and to be as open as you can to weekly subscriptions is something we see a lot of success with in some regions but not too many developers using it especially out of the us in addition to prepaid plans.

435
00:37:20,160 --> 00:37:27,920
So it's not really a subscription, but another way to market a subscription to a user so that they don't feel that burden of I got to commit for a whole year to this product.

436
00:37:28,480 --> 00:37:41,200
We were talking about this earlier and it's the kind of thing that might actually work really well for AI apps because they do have such a high cost of goods is that instead of offering a seven day free trial, maybe you offer a seven day pass.

437
00:37:42,240 --> 00:37:43,040
That's two bucks.

438
00:37:43,760 --> 00:37:51,040
And if there's a really low barrier to entry and the user sees a lot of value, then you kind of get them in at this lower price.

439
00:37:51,120 --> 00:37:53,040
And then if they do see a ton of value, that's the.

440
00:37:53,440 --> 00:38:02,000
on-ramp to the subscription rather than just offering a free trial, which may end up being very expensive to deliver, especially when your cost is high.

441
00:38:02,480 --> 00:38:05,680
And so, yeah, thinking through some of these, and that's a fairly low hanging free.

442
00:38:05,760 --> 00:38:11,040
Like, it's not that hard to create a seven day pass and offer that instead of a seven day free trial.

443
00:38:11,040 --> 00:38:14,080
Like, there's a lot of this kind of experimenting in pricing and packaging.

444
00:38:14,480 --> 00:38:20,320
I did experiment in my weather app, just showing the monthly price as a default.

445
00:38:20,480 --> 00:38:24,960
So in my app, you just see one price and then there's a button to see all the plans.

446
00:38:25,440 --> 00:38:32,240
So I did an experiment, seeing the four dollar a month plan, does that increase conversion compared to seeing the $40 a year plan?

447
00:38:32,640 --> 00:38:38,640
It turns out 20% increase in paid subscribers by them just seeing $40 anchoring, right?

448
00:38:38,880 --> 00:38:40,720
They see $4, it's like, oh, that's cheap.

449
00:38:40,800 --> 00:38:40,880
Yeah.

450
00:38:41,200 --> 00:38:47,200
Funny enough, I ran the experiment through RevenueCat and I've been tracking the data and I'm going to actually lose money.

451
00:38:47,600 --> 00:38:53,680
So even though I got a 20% increase in conversion, those monthly subscribers are churning.

452
00:38:54,240 --> 00:39:08,560
So I switched it back to the $40 plan because that is going to maximize revenue, but it was just super low hanging fruit experiment and to see that lower price point was really attractive and did lead to more paying users, even though it didn't maximize LTV for me.

453
00:39:08,880 --> 00:39:09,040
Right.

454
00:39:09,280 --> 00:39:11,200
And those are the kind of things that people should be experiencing.

455
00:39:12,000 --> 00:39:12,320
I love that.

456
00:39:12,400 --> 00:39:19,600
And I love that you took the time to look at the full analysis of the data, how it impacted your LTV, and were open to just trying out new things.

457
00:39:19,840 --> 00:39:23,040
I think that's exactly what we're encouraging the ecosystem to do.

458
00:39:23,280 --> 00:39:30,720
It's not so much to absolutely have as many tiers as possible and consumables and a virtual coin currency of your own.

459
00:39:31,200 --> 00:39:37,680
It's really getting into that habit, that muscle of continuously experimenting, tracking what's working and what's not based on your goals.

460
00:39:38,160 --> 00:39:52,160
Again, and if you are global, which I hope many who are listening are, because that's the beauty of mobile development, being very aware that what works in one market or works for one set of users, especially on Android, is very likely to not work as well outside that group.

461
00:39:52,480 --> 00:39:55,440
So just to be much more nimble and test as much as you can.

462
00:39:55,680 --> 00:39:55,760
Yeah.

463
00:39:56,560 --> 00:40:05,760
And your team has been thinking a lot about and sharing about, and we talked about a kind of framework to think about these experiments through.

464
00:40:06,160 --> 00:40:06,240
Yeah.

465
00:40:07,040 --> 00:40:10,000
is maximizing the points along the demand curve.

466
00:40:10,320 --> 00:40:16,080
Like some people are willing to play a pay a small amount, but there are a lot of people willing to pay a small amount.

467
00:40:16,240 --> 00:40:16,400
Right.

468
00:40:16,560 --> 00:40:18,960
And there's a small number of people who are willing to pay a lot.

469
00:40:19,200 --> 00:40:19,360
Right.

470
00:40:19,840 --> 00:40:21,200
And kind of finding ways.

471
00:40:21,520 --> 00:40:33,600
So as you're experimenting with these hybrid monetization and different pricing and maybe a paid trial instead of a free trial, it's looking for ways to maximize all the points on that demand curve.

472
00:40:33,680 --> 00:40:36,080
Like meeting people where they are for their willingness to pay.

473
00:40:36,320 --> 00:40:36,480
Right.

474
00:40:36,800 --> 00:40:40,640
And a lot of us probably are leaving money on the table not experimenting on the high end of things.

475
00:40:40,800 --> 00:40:44,240
It's like offering a super premium tier, offering additional ways to pay.

476
00:40:44,480 --> 00:40:44,560
Yeah.

477
00:40:44,880 --> 00:40:45,920
We've seen a lot in our data.

478
00:40:47,440 --> 00:40:56,480
The majority of users who will purchase in an app, including subscriptions and beyond, tend to wait over a year before they make their purchase.

479
00:40:56,960 --> 00:41:00,400
That is a very common flow, so which was very surprising to us.

480
00:41:00,480 --> 00:41:00,880
Absolutely.

481
00:41:01,120 --> 00:41:01,200
Yeah.

482
00:41:02,080 --> 00:41:08,400
We had assumed like, oh, everyone's just gonna wake up, install their app and then immediately get convinced by the value proposition and purchase.

483
00:41:08,560 --> 00:41:21,040
And I think what that insight told us, and it does differ a little bit by vertical and type of app use case, but it's generally, there's a big audience out there that won't be convinced in the first few days of your app that they're willing to pay, but they will be one day.

484
00:41:21,360 --> 00:41:22,880
And that data is exactly what we see.

485
00:41:23,440 --> 00:41:35,200
So yeah, exactly to your point, picking up as many signals as you can through your own understanding of what your app offers, your own audience analytics, and then thinking through what might be the right offer for this user based on their patterns.

486
00:41:35,440 --> 00:41:49,200
Are they a merry coming in every now and then, somebody who might be shopping around for different apps in this use case, that you might have a different approach for that kind of user versus like you said, somebody might be a power user, really engaged, and you might be leaving value on the table with them.

487
00:41:49,360 --> 00:41:50,240
You're not offering them enough.

488
00:41:50,880 --> 00:41:53,920
Maybe they're a monthly subscriber, but there's more that they're very willing to pay for.

489
00:41:54,320 --> 00:41:59,280
So absolutely, I think there's this mindset that I either have to get some someone to pay or they're free.

490
00:41:59,680 --> 00:42:02,880
And if I don't do that in the first couple of weeks, then I'm missing out.

491
00:42:03,040 --> 00:42:04,160
I've got a low conversion rate.

492
00:42:04,240 --> 00:42:06,960
And I think we're seeing that, that's kind of maybe the old mobile world.

493
00:42:07,200 --> 00:42:14,960
And I think the more sophisticated developers that we're seeing greater success are thinking much longer term and bigger picture in terms of the segments of their audience.

494
00:42:15,440 --> 00:42:21,360
And probably again, stage, you know, an early stage app maybe does need to push really hard on that early monetization.

495
00:42:21,920 --> 00:42:23,760
And maybe they can't play that long game.

496
00:42:24,480 --> 00:42:26,240
But then to see that that is an opportunity.

497
00:42:26,480 --> 00:42:30,720
I was shocked when I saw those numbers that so many people do subscribe after a year.

498
00:42:31,760 --> 00:42:32,800
So freemium can be.

499
00:42:33,200 --> 00:42:34,640
and is a very powerful strategy.

500
00:42:34,880 --> 00:42:40,320
Get the habituation, show them some value and eventually bring them into the paying fold.

501
00:42:41,280 --> 00:42:41,680
Exactly.

502
00:42:42,160 --> 00:42:46,720
One of the things we've been talking about but haven't really gone super deep in is consumables.

503
00:42:46,880 --> 00:42:50,880
Like, in some ways it's kind of the ultimate way to meet people along the demand curve.

504
00:42:51,040 --> 00:43:02,880
And I feel like Tinder, I've talked about this a lot as a great example of that is that those consumables really give the people who want to pay more, who are super engaged, who can pay more, the ability to pay more.

505
00:43:03,520 --> 00:43:05,360
But this probably not the best fit for every app.

506
00:43:05,680 --> 00:43:12,960
So how is your team seeing consumables be used most effectively to kind of meet people at different points in that demand curve?

507
00:43:13,120 --> 00:43:13,200
Yeah.

508
00:43:13,280 --> 00:43:14,800
And you're spot on the Tinder.

509
00:43:15,040 --> 00:43:24,560
A lot of dating apps have seen great success with both IAP and the hybrid model where if you're a subscriber, you might even get unique access to certain consumables that are a good fit for you.

510
00:43:25,040 --> 00:43:29,360
You know, we are seeing more, it's funny, you say that maybe some people would think that's not a fit for their business.

511
00:43:30,080 --> 00:43:32,080
And I'm going to say something radical right now.

512
00:43:32,960 --> 00:43:33,600
So bear with me.

513
00:43:33,840 --> 00:43:35,520
I think it could be a fit for anyone's business.

514
00:43:35,760 --> 00:43:39,040
And I think there's something out there that is a good fit for consumables.

515
00:43:39,520 --> 00:44:18,240
It's just about thinking through what's the right way for you to do it and when to your point on maturity that's not something we'd recommend to like if you're very early stage and you're a subscription model year or two and that might not be the right time to think about adding a whole bunch of IAP and I'll give an example I think what we've seen out of a lot of developers in Korea and Japan that are in the web novel, webtoon or comics space huge industry and actually it's really growing in the US we see a lot of young Americans loving that kind of content and using a lot of those apps on their phone and that industry used to be a little bit more subscription heavy and similar to many other content industries kind of this model where you're subscribing and getting whatever it is your access for a monthly period or an annual period.

516
00:44:18,560 --> 00:44:34,160
And what we found is that many WebTunes and WebNovel developers are experimenting with their own virtual currencies with a hybrid of IAP and subscriptions and seeing really good success, especially when thinking through, like you said earlier, the high value users who might have a higher appetite for certain IP.

517
00:44:34,720 --> 00:44:41,600
There's more they can offer there and they have that content and they can even be creative with what they source from their own creators, the artists.

518
00:44:41,760 --> 00:44:43,440
So I think there's so many ways.

519
00:44:44,160 --> 00:44:45,840
any business could approach IAP.

520
00:44:46,080 --> 00:44:50,240
It is something to think about when you're in a more mature stage if you're a subscriptions business because it is complicated.

521
00:44:50,640 --> 00:44:53,840
We're very bullish on learning from other industries.

522
00:44:54,000 --> 00:44:57,520
You know, obviously gaming is by far the most IAP centric industry.

523
00:44:58,000 --> 00:45:14,880
I think there's a lot apps can learn from both obviously from gaming but also from dating from comics from web novels who and social apps have also been experimenting a lot more with IAP because it can be a really good way to engage your valuable users and to offer them more chances to build that trust and that buy-in with your app.

524
00:45:15,280 --> 00:45:16,880
As you said earlier, people want to pay.

525
00:45:16,960 --> 00:45:19,040
We're seeing that propensity increase over time.

526
00:45:19,680 --> 00:45:22,960
So giving them the options to do that in a way that works for them is a smart thing to do.

527
00:45:23,280 --> 00:46:05,040
So speaking of gaming, you know, a lot of people talk about gamification of apps and we have seen huge example like duolingo is a great example of bringing a level of gamification into the app but you also mentioned like tipping and coins and collectibles and stuff like that i actually met yesterday i might end up having him on the podcast he was a hoot to talk to the founder of run pee the app where tells you when in a movie you can go pee where you're not going to miss a big plot twist or something and he actually said they moved to fully tipping and he's making more money than ever and that people just love the app so much and he presents it in a very like you know help us create this content and he was saying they get surprisingly large tips even.

528
00:46:05,600 --> 00:46:12,640
And so point being with this whole conversation has been about like diversifying monetization and gamification, tipping coins, collectibles.

529
00:46:12,880 --> 00:46:14,400
Like there's so many ways to do that.

530
00:46:14,800 --> 00:46:17,600
So any low hanging fruit there that you think people should be exploring?

531
00:46:17,920 --> 00:46:25,920
Yeah, we are seeing huge growth in tipping, whether that's through a coin economy or just, yeah, some IAP that you can offer at various price points.

532
00:46:26,320 --> 00:46:28,560
And we're seeing it mostly in livestreaming and social.

533
00:46:29,120 --> 00:46:30,960
And to your point though, we're seeing that expand.

534
00:46:31,280 --> 00:46:34,880
We've noticed this trend where creator economy apps really started.

535
00:46:35,120 --> 00:47:13,120
The most fast growing ones tended to be in video creation so youtube and discord tick tock that kind of app and we're now seeing that model expand more and more into different areas and industries for example there's this trend that we're seeing coming out of a lot of great or china based apps called drama shorts which is these short form videos they're definitely worth checking out some of these apps they're very fun to watch very engaging almost like the days of our lives style like dramatic and a lot of their content and their monetization models are akin to games you know a lot of ip a lot of time-based things and on tipping you're absolutely right that we're seeing more developers experiment with tipping outside of the traditional social live streaming.

536
00:47:13,360 --> 00:47:17,440
I mean, it definitely is something to, I think, continue experimenting with.

537
00:47:17,680 --> 00:47:22,320
It kind of depends on the problem you're trying to solve for the user back to the problem and the value that they're going to get.

538
00:47:22,720 --> 00:47:25,680
But absolutely, that's something that we think has got a huge bright future.

539
00:47:25,920 --> 00:47:28,720
We're seeing growth in these types of business models at a faster rate.

540
00:47:29,120 --> 00:47:44,240
And that's part of the reason we announced also yesterday that we're increasing our price limits, I think, to like 999,99, because we know that there's demand and some users are absolutely very into these creators and have a lot of love for them and the content they're making and want to tip.

541
00:47:44,640 --> 00:47:45,920
So it is a really important space.

542
00:47:46,080 --> 00:47:53,680
It's fast growing and I think creator economies have this beautiful symbiosis where often it's creating a space for these smaller creators.

543
00:47:53,920 --> 00:48:04,800
We call them professional user generated content where you're not going to have your goal is not even necessarily to become the next whatever it might be, you know, major studio, major artist, major et cetera.

544
00:48:05,040 --> 00:48:06,400
Sometimes it is, but sometimes it's not.

545
00:48:06,560 --> 00:48:15,840
Sometimes you do want to remain a kind of a professional state but in a smaller scale and this allows you to have a business and run that business not just for video creators but for all types of creators out there.

546
00:48:16,320 --> 00:48:22,960
The last topic I wanted to bring up from the white paper, and it was really cool again that you all shared a white paper and shared a bunch of data.

547
00:48:23,200 --> 00:48:42,800
And I know the team's looking to share more and more data over time, but you mentioned secondary product market fit and the fact that apps that find solid secondary product market fit have almost triple the minutes with their daily active users and almost double the daily active user to monthly active user ratio.

548
00:48:43,360 --> 00:48:45,440
And we saw this with Calm, a great example.

549
00:48:45,760 --> 00:48:47,600
Again, Pioneer, big app in the space.

550
00:48:48,320 --> 00:48:57,280
is that when they expanded from meditation into sleep stories, it wasn't this dramatic like bolt on a whole other feature set, but it was a very.

551
00:48:57,920 --> 00:49:04,400
natural extension of the product, but it was a very strong secondary product market fit that kind of unlocked new growth for them.

552
00:49:05,040 --> 00:49:13,600
I haven't talked to Calm and gotten any data on this or anything, but from what we see broadly, it seems like that created a new inflection point in their growth.

553
00:49:13,840 --> 00:49:18,480
And I would imagine similarly, they probably saw increased engagement, more daily active users.

554
00:49:18,960 --> 00:49:20,000
You know, there's a trigger, right?

555
00:49:20,160 --> 00:49:22,160
We were talking about earlier, it's like a trigger.

556
00:49:22,320 --> 00:49:23,120
I need to go to sleep.

557
00:49:23,280 --> 00:49:25,120
Oh, now I'm going to use Calm to sleep.

558
00:49:25,200 --> 00:49:28,960
Whereas maybe meditation, it's like, oh, I know I should meditate, but I don't have time today.

559
00:49:29,600 --> 00:49:36,080
And so that was a way to not only create secondary product market fit but create this secondary trigger to bring people back in the app.

560
00:49:36,480 --> 00:49:39,920
So what are you seeing be successful in this secondary product market fit?

561
00:49:40,240 --> 00:49:41,200
Yeah, I think absolutely.

562
00:49:41,360 --> 00:49:52,080
I love that, you know, this is data that we saw corroborated by what our instincts were really telling us that like if you I think what Khan did really well and John Tam, their CPO, I think speaks about this really thoughtfully.

563
00:49:52,720 --> 00:49:58,560
They're out to solve a set of user problems that actually have some common symptoms potentially where, you know, I'm not sleeping well.

564
00:49:58,720 --> 00:49:59,760
Maybe I have some anxiety.

565
00:50:00,000 --> 00:50:00,720
I'm a new parent.

566
00:50:00,880 --> 00:50:03,120
I'm not sleeping well because I have young kids.

567
00:50:03,600 --> 00:50:11,520
They've continued to think very holistically about that instead of thinking, you you know, another way to think about your product is I'm a live streaming app or I'm a meditation app.

568
00:50:11,760 --> 00:50:16,240
And that can be a lot more restrictive and isn't actually what your users are coming to you for.

569
00:50:16,480 --> 00:50:17,520
They're coming because they have a problem.

570
00:50:17,920 --> 00:50:20,240
There's a great quote that Andy Dunn often uses.

571
00:50:20,400 --> 00:50:25,200
I think it's attributed to Peter Drucker originally that the consumer rarely buys what you think you sell.

572
00:50:26,080 --> 00:50:41,920
And I think it's a very accurate trend that we see in the apps in the store that often the reason that somebody's coming to your app has a multitude of ways you can solve for that problem and to not feel restricted to I'm only going to offer streaming video or I'm only going to offer a social text-based interaction from a design.

573
00:50:42,640 --> 00:50:47,840
perspective, we're very supportive of clarity and like a clear user proposition and clear onboarding, all of the above.

574
00:50:48,000 --> 00:50:49,280
And I think Khan does that very well.

575
00:50:49,680 --> 00:50:50,560
But yeah, absolutely.

576
00:50:50,640 --> 00:50:57,280
We do see that if you can solve that problem in multiple ways and offer more diversity of content on how to do that, because not everyone's the same.

577
00:50:57,520 --> 00:50:59,600
And some people might want to listen to something without video.

578
00:50:59,840 --> 00:51:00,960
Some people might want only video.

579
00:51:01,280 --> 00:51:02,480
Some people might want a transcript.

580
00:51:02,560 --> 00:51:07,280
Like if you can be more, again, back to diversity of different users and different ways of interacting, you can be.

581
00:51:07,760 --> 00:51:10,800
big picture thinking about the problem you're solving, we see a lot more success.

582
00:51:11,040 --> 00:51:18,080
So yeah, we see that across the board of every type of user behavior that if you offer more than one way to solve that problem, you'll see better success.

583
00:51:18,320 --> 00:51:19,040
Yeah, very cool.

